{"id": "2026-08-19-science-cuprate-superconducts-in-a-single-copper-oxygen", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Cuprate superconducts in a single copper-oxygen plane", "summary": "A cuprate thinned to one CuO2 plane still superconducts, isolating the 2D limit of high-Tc physics.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10857-1", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Cuprates — copper-oxide ceramics that superconduct (carry current with zero resistance) at unusually high temperatures — are built from stacked CuO₂ (copper–oxygen) planes, and a long-standing question is how few of those planes the effect actually needs. Borrowing the top-down, sticky-tape exfoliation trick that made graphene, a Fudan-led team thinned a cuprate all the way down to a single CuO₂ plane and still measured superconductivity, isolating the truly two-dimensional limit of the problem. Earlier work had shown the essential physics fits within two planes; pushing to one strips the system to what may be its minimal ingredient.", "why": "If one copper–oxygen layer is enough to superconduct, theories of high-temperature superconductivity can be tested against the barest possible system — a cleaner route toward understanding, and someday designing, better superconductors.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-cuprate-superconducts-in-a-single-copper-oxygen", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["Fudan University", "University of British Columbia", "Zhejiang University"], "entities": ["Fudan University", "University of British Columbia"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-graphene-device-measures-one-third-of-an-electro", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Graphene device measures one-third of an electron", "summary": "An EPFL antidot device reads the e/3 charge of fractional quantum Hall quasiparticles directly.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03412-2", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["science", "switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "In the fractional quantum Hall effect — what emerges when electrons are confined to two dimensions in an intense magnetic field — the electron fluid gives rise to \"quasiparticles\" that act as if they carry only a fraction of an electron's charge. Measuring that fraction cleanly is hard, because the usual interferometer designs entangle bulk and edge contributions. The EPFL-led team instead built an antidot — a controllable bump in the device's potential landscape that behaves as a tunable artificial impurity — into a graphene sample and read the charge of quasiparticles tunnelling through it, directly extracting values such as e/3 (one-third of the elementary charge).", "why": "A clean way to weigh fractional charges is a prerequisite for probing these quasiparticles' exotic quantum statistics — the same physics being courted for topological quantum computing. The lead group is Swiss.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-graphene-device-measures-one-third-of-an-electro", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["EPFL", "Oxford", "NIMS"], "entities": ["EPFL", "fractional quantum Hall effect"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-chemists-walk-nitrogen-around-a-pyridine-ring", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Chemists walk nitrogen around a pyridine ring", "summary": "A sequence of nitrogen insertion and deletion transposes a pyridine's nitrogen, relocating substituents.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11006-4", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Pyridines — six-membered aromatic rings carrying a single nitrogen, ubiquitous in drug molecules — behave very differently depending on where a substituent sits relative to that nitrogen, and normally each \"positional isomer\" (same atoms, different arrangement) must be built from scratch. The IBS/KAIST team report a way to reorganise the ring itself: a controlled sequence of nitrogen insertion and deletion transposes the nitrogen to a new position, relocating the attached groups without changing their identity. A companion paper from Cambridge performs related \"skeletal editing\" on tertiary benzylamines, rewriting their core carbon–nitrogen connectivity late in a synthesis (Nature, University of Cambridge).", "why": "Skeletal editing lets chemists remodel a molecule's core in the final steps instead of redesigning a whole synthesis — a faster lane for exploring drug-like variants around a promising scaffold.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-chemists-walk-nitrogen-around-a-pyridine-ring", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["Institute for Basic Science", "KAIST"], "entities": ["Institute for Basic Science", "KAIST"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-blood-test-flags-immunotherapy-benefit-in-lung-c", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Blood test flags immunotherapy benefit in lung cancer", "summary": "Circulating-tumour-DNA clearance tracks who gains from perioperative nivolumab in resectable NSCLC.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10925-6", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "In resectable non-small-cell lung cancer (a lung tumour still removable by surgery), the CheckMate 77T trial had already shown that giving the immunotherapy nivolumab before and after surgery improves event-free survival; this analysis asks who benefits. Following circulating tumour DNA (ctDNA — fragments of tumour genome shed into the blood) in the biomarker-evaluable patients (41% of those randomised), the team found ctDNA detectable in 85% before treatment and 92% by the end of pre-surgery dosing, and that clearing it during that window tracked with benefit.", "why": "A blood test that signals whether immunotherapy is working before the operation could help spare non-responders unnecessary treatment and flag who needs more — though this is a retrospective subgroup analysis, not yet a decision rule.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-blood-test-flags-immunotherapy-benefit-in-lung-c", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["MD Anderson Cancer Center", "Memorial Sloan Kettering", "McGill University"], "entities": ["nivolumab", "CheckMate 77T", "MD Anderson Cancer Center"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-in-vivo-crispr-screen-reads-t-cells-inside-tumou", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "In-vivo CRISPR screen reads T cells inside tumours", "summary": "A mouse model recovers human T cells from solid tumours, enabling genome-wide in-vivo CRISPR screens.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10906-9", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science", "health"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Finding genetic tweaks that help immune cells attack solid tumours is a central goal of cell therapy, but the genes that matter inside a living tumour often stay hidden in lab-dish (in-vitro) screens. The UCSF/Gladstone team built a mouse model that efficiently recovers human T cells from solid tumours, making genome-wide CRISPR screens — systematically switching off each gene to see which alter T-cell behaviour — feasible with only a handful of animals. The tumour-infiltrating cells showed the classic signs of \"exhaustion,\" the dysfunctional state that blunts immunotherapy, giving the screen a realistic setting to map its drivers.", "why": "A screen that mirrors the hostile tumour environment can surface engineering targets that dish experiments miss — grist for the next generation of CAR-T and tumour-infiltrating-lymphocyte therapies.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-in-vivo-crispr-screen-reads-t-cells-inside-tumou", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["UCSF", "Gladstone Institutes"], "entities": ["UCSF", "Gladstone Institutes", "CRISPR"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-distant-galaxies-hide-mass-in-dim-dwarf-stars", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Distant galaxies hide mass in dim dwarf stars", "summary": "JWST spectra of z~0.7 galaxies show a bottom-heavy stellar mix, implying extra hidden stellar mass.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02932-4", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A galaxy's stellar mass is inferred from its light, but that conversion hinges on the initial mass function (IMF) — the assumed spread of star masses at birth, usually copied from the Milky Way. Faint low-mass stars dominate the mass yet contribute little light, so the low-mass end is normally guessed, not measured. Using ultra-deep JWST spectra of nine massive quiescent galaxies at redshift z ≈ 0.7 (light that left them roughly 6 billion years ago), the Leiden-led team constrained that low-mass end directly and found the IMF \"bottom-heavy\" — richer in dwarf stars than the Milky Way's, implying more hidden stellar mass than standard assumptions give.", "why": "JWST has turned up early galaxies that look almost too massive to exist so soon; a bottom-heavy IMF shows galaxies really can carry extra unseen stellar mass, which bears on how the masses — and the assembly speed — of the earliest galaxies are read. The measurement is at intermediate redshift, so it informs rather than settles the high-redshift debate.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-distant-galaxies-hide-mass-in-dim-dwarf-stars", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["Leiden University", "Carnegie Institution for Science", "Yale"], "entities": ["JWST", "Leiden University"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-first-globular-cluster-stream-found-beyond-milky", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "First globular-cluster stream found beyond Milky Way", "summary": "A tidal stream from a globular cluster is found around an ultra-diffuse galaxy, a new dark-matter probe.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10878-w", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Stellar streams — thin ribbons of stars pulled from a cluster by a galaxy's tides — trace the host's gravity, and so its dark matter. Until now, globular-cluster streams were known only in the Milky Way. In deep Hubble Space Telescope imaging of the ultra-diffuse galaxy UGC 9050-Dw1 (a galaxy as sprawling as the Milky Way but with very few stars), a Copenhagen-led team found evidence for the first such stream around another galaxy, and used generative modelling of its shape to weigh the progenitor cluster.", "why": "Extragalactic streams open a new way to map dark matter well beyond the Milky Way — including the puzzling ultra-diffuse galaxies whose dark-matter content is fiercely debated.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-first-globular-cluster-stream-found-beyond-milky", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["University of Copenhagen", "DTU", "Princeton"], "entities": ["Hubble Space Telescope", "UGC 9050-Dw1", "University of Copenhagen"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-deimos-shaped-by-one-glancing-impact", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Deimos shaped by one glancing impact", "summary": "Simulations tie Deimos's smooth surface and south-polar basin to a single subcatastrophic impact.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02956-w", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["science", "switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Deimos, the smaller, 12-km Martian moon, is oddly smooth and coated in fine regolith (loose surface dust), unlike its heavily cratered sibling Phobos, and its shape is dominated by one large south-polar depression. Simulations by the Bern-led team show a single oblique impact from a roughly 320-metre projectile can quantitatively reproduce that depression, the global dust blanket, and finer features such as bright streaks and infilled craters — a \"subcatastrophic\" hit that remodelled the moon without breaking it apart.", "why": "Tying Deimos's odd surface to a single impact sharpens the science targets for JAXA's planned Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) mission to the two moons. The lead group is Swiss.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-deimos-shaped-by-one-glancing-impact", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["University of Bern", "Vrije Universiteit Brussel", "Royal Observatory of Belgium"], "entities": ["Deimos", "Mars", "University of Bern"]}
{"id": "2026-08-19-science-milky-way-s-oldest-known-merger-revealed", "date": "2026-08-19", "stream": "science", "headline": "Milky Way's oldest known merger revealed", "summary": "Dated globular clusters point to a major Milky Way merger 1.8 Gyr before Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus.", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02931-5", "source_domain": "nature.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["science"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Milky Way grew by devouring smaller galaxies, and the largest early merger securely known — Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus (a dwarf galaxy that fell in) — dates to about 10 billion years ago. Using globular clusters as tracers and a new method to date them, the INAF Bologna-led team found the clusters split into three distinct age–metallicity sequences: one native to the proto-Milky Way, one from Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus, and one pointing to an earlier, previously unrecognised major accretion event some 1.8 billion years before it.", "why": "Each identified merger is a chapter in the Galaxy's origin story; finding an even older one pushes back the clock on when the Milky Way began assembling in earnest.", "thread_id": "2026-08-19-science-milky-way-s-oldest-known-merger-revealed", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-19", "event_date": "2026-08-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["INAF", "University of Bologna", "University of Groningen"], "entities": ["Milky Way", "Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus", "INAF"]}
